  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 225 PM CST Sun Nov 1 2026\n\nFog has finally cleared earlier this morning as warm air is \nadvected into the region along a warm front. For the afternoon,\nthe blob of rain over northeast Minnesota this morning will\ncontinue its trek northeast through the Arrowhead this afternoon,\nthough any rain will be brief and any accumulations are \nexpected to be little to none. Aside from this passing moisture,\nexpect dry and mild conditions across the Northland for the \nrest of the afternoon. Strong warm air advection will push \ntemperatures well into the 60s and a few around 70 for inland \nlocations. Clouds will thicken across the area again tonight \nahead of the next system.\n\nRain showers will spread across the region on Monday through\nTuesday as a deep upper level trough and subsequent large low \npressure system move into the central United States. An \nanomalous moisture plume forecast to be above the 90th\nclimatological percentile will bring a soaking rainfall to the \narea. Quick pressure falls ahead of the system will lead to \nbreezy conditions, with southeast winds gusting between 20 and \n30 mph by Monday afternoon. The heaviest rainfall rates are \nanticipated during the Monday afternoon and evening hours. There\nis still quite a spread of QPF values for this system, This is \nespecially true for the Brainerd Lakes area and within an \norographic enhancement band along the North Shore, where \naccumulations between 1.5-2.5 inches are possible. Initial QPF \nvalues for Tuesday seemed to have jumped dramatically compared \nto previous runs, so we opted to try and tamper them down a bit \nto get QPF values to better match with other ensemble guidance. \nEven so, most of the area has a very high chance (80%+) of \nseeing at least an inch of rain.\n\nIsolated embedded thunderstorms are possible on Tuesday as some\nweak instability gets added to the mix, though severe weather is\nnot anticipated. Light precipitation will gradually taper off \nfrom west to east through Wednesday afternoon as the center of \nthe low pressure system briefly stalls over the Northland and \nthen slowly departs into the Great Lakes, leaving cool and \ncloudy conditions into Thursday.\n\nHigh pressure will build into the Northland for late Thursday \nand Friday, bringing an end to the wet pattern. Drier conditions\nwill prevail for the end of the week, though mostly cloudy \nskies will linger. Cooler air filtering in behind the departing \nsystem will return our temperatures to seasonal norms. Expect \nhighs back near normal into the 40s with overnight lows \ndropping into the upper 20s and lower 30s heading into the \nupcoming weekend.",
